{% extends "layout.njk" %}
{% import "macros.njk" as m %}
{% set title = "Studio site sections" %}
{% set slug = "patterns-studio" %}

{% block content %}
<h1>Studio site sections</h1>
<p class="lede">
  Sections from a small studio's marketing site, modelled on
  <a href="https://sparkstonepdx.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">sparkstonepdx.com</a>.
  Each one is a few components and a layout, and the notes under it say which
  piece is doing which job. The rules behind them are on
  <a href="./composition.html">Composition</a>.
</p>

<section id="header">
  <h2>Header and hero</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>.navbar</code> for the site links, then a hero: one heading, one
    supporting sentence, one filled button.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<header class="navbar">
  <div class="navbar-start">
    <strong>Sparkstone</strong>
  </div>
  <nav class="navbar-end">
    <a href="#" class="link">Case Studies</a>
    <a href="#" class="link">Products</a>
    <a href="#" class="link">Blog</a>
    <a href="#" class="btn">Book a call</a>
  </nav>
</header>

<section class="prose" style="max-width: 40rem; padding: calc(var(--padding) * 3) 0">
  <h1>Custom software and websites for medical practices, law firms, and specialty contractors in Portland.</h1>
  <p class="text-secondary">
    I'm Adam. I run Sparkstone, a one-person software studio. I build the
    systems your business actually runs on.
  </p>
  <a href="#" class="btn">Book a call</a>
  <p class="text-secondary" style="margin-top: var(--padding)">
    <small>Featured on Behind the Creative Curtain, NarwhalPod, and CreativeMornings</small>
  </p>
</section>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-hero", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Layout:</strong> <code>.navbar-start</code> and <code>.navbar-end</code> push apart. The links and the button share the end group's gap, so they line up with no extra classes.</li>
    <li><strong>Actions:</strong> the header's call to action and the hero's are the same <code>.btn</code>. Nothing else on screen is filled, so the eye has one place to go.</li>
    <li><strong>Text:</strong> <code>.prose</code> sits on the hero column, not the page, so it spaces the heading, lede and button without touching the navbar.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="services">
  <h2>Services</h2>
  <p>
    Several offers of equal weight. They sit on <code>.card-border</code> so none
    looks featured over the others.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<section class="prose">
  <h2>Services</h2>
  <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(13rem, 1fr)); gap: var(--padding)">
    <div class="card-border">
      <h3>Custom applications</h3>
      <p class="text-secondary">The internal tool or portal you can't buy off the shelf.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-border">
      <h3>Project rescue</h3>
      <p class="text-secondary">Take over a build that stalled, or clean up after one.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-border">
      <h3>Websites</h3>
      <p class="text-secondary">A site your staff can edit themselves, on a monthly retainer.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-services", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Text:</strong> one <code>.prose</code> on the section spaces the section heading and every card's heading and paragraph. The grid is a plain <code>div</code>, and <code>.prose</code> does not space a <code>div</code>, so the columns stay tight.</li>
    <li><strong>Surface:</strong> <code>.card-border</code> takes <code>--padding</code> for its inside, so setting <code>--padding</code> on the section resizes all three cards together.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="case-studies">
  <h2>Case study cards</h2>
  <p>
    Each card is one link, so the anchor is the card. A badge says what kind of
    work it was, and a <code>.link</code> span at the bottom says what clicking does.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr)); gap: var(--padding)">
  <a href="#" class="semantic card prose">
    <span class="badge">Open source</span>
    <h3 style="margin-top: var(--padding)">The Map I Never Meant to Build</h3>
    <p class="text-secondary">Sixty thousand views, three neighborhoods asking to borrow it, and a little map that got better every spring.</p>
    <span class="link">Read the case study</span>
  </a>
  <a href="#" class="semantic card prose">
    <span class="badge">Website</span>
    <h3 style="margin-top: var(--padding)">A Library That Fits in a Free Little Library</h3>
    <p class="text-secondary">A zine catalog, a printing guide, and an events page, built from a pile of draft copy.</p>
    <span class="link">Read the case study</span>
  </a>
  <a href="#" class="semantic card prose">
    <span class="badge">Product</span>
    <h3 style="margin-top: var(--padding)">Liveframe</h3>
    <p class="text-secondary">Guests scan a QR code to share photos, messages and polls in real time, with no app to install.</p>
    <span class="link">See the product</span>
  </a>
</div>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-case-studies", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Actions:</strong> <code>.semantic</code> removes the link's own styling and <code>.card</code>, later in the cascade, puts the surface back. The grid lays each anchor out as a block, so it needs no <code>display</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Spacing:</strong> nothing has a top margin, so the heading under a badge needs one. It uses <code>var(--padding)</code> so it moves with the card's padding.</li>
    <li><strong>Text:</strong> the <code>.link</code> span is styled text, not a second link. Nested anchors are invalid, and one target per card is what makes it a card.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="testimonial">
  <h2>Testimonial</h2>
  <p>
    A quote is prose: <code>.prose</code> gives the <code>blockquote</code> its
    rule and padding. The attribution is supporting text.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<figure class="card prose">
  <blockquote>
    We are a tiny team with big dreams of making zines more accessible for
    everyone. Adam showed sincere interest in our project and listened to our
    ideas.
  </blockquote>
  <figcaption class="text-secondary">Little Zine Revolution</figcaption>
</figure>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-testimonial", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Surface:</strong> a <code>.card</code> rather than a bordered box, because a testimonial is meant to be looked at. The quote's <code>--line</code> rule reads against <code>--surface</code> as well as against the page.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="newsletter">
  <h2>Newsletter band</h2>
  <p>
    An input and a button side by side. The input keeps its own bottom margin,
    so the row aligns them to the end of the label rather than the middle.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<section class="card" data-color-scheme="dark">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Newsletter</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary">Subscribe to be the first to hear about what I do next.</p>
  </div>
  <form class="flex flip" style="align-items: end; row-gap: 0">
    <label class="label" style="flex: 1">
      <p>Email</p>
      <input class="input" type="email" placeholder="you@example.com" />
    </label>
    <button class="btn" style="margin-bottom: 1rem">Subscribe</button>
  </form>
</section>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-newsletter", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Boundary:</strong> <code>data-color-scheme="dark"</code> on the card turns this one band dark on a light page. The input, button and muted text all follow, because they read derived colours.</li>
    <li><strong>Spacing:</strong> the label and input carry their bottom margins into the row. Giving the button the same <code>1rem</code> lines its bottom edge up with the input's. <code>.flip</code> stacks them on a phone, and <code>row-gap: 0</code> leaves the stacked spacing to those margins.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="contact">
  <h2>Contact form</h2>
  <p>
    A form is a stack of labels. Fieldsets group them, and
    <code>.card-actions</code> closes the card with the submit button.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<form class="card">
  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Book a call</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary">Tell me a little about the project.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="flex flip" style="row-gap: 0">
    <label class="label" style="flex: 1">
      <p>Name</p>
      <input class="input" autocomplete="name" />
    </label>
    <label class="label" style="flex: 1">
      <p>Email</p>
      <input class="input" type="email" autocomplete="email" />
    </label>
  </div>
  <label class="label">
    <p>What kind of business?</p>
    <select class="select">
      <option>Medical practice</option>
      <option>Law firm</option>
      <option>Specialty contractor</option>
      <option>Something else</option>
    </select>
  </label>
  <label class="label">
    <p>What do you need?</p>
    <textarea class="textarea" rows="4"></textarea>
    <small>A few sentences is plenty.</small>
  </label>
  <footer class="card-actions flex" style="justify-content: end">
    <button class="btn">Send</button>
  </footer>
</form>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-contact", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Layout:</strong> two short fields share a <code>.flex .flip</code> row and stack on phones. Each label takes <code>flex: 1</code>; the controls inside are already full width. <code>row-gap: 0</code> keeps the stacked labels from getting the row's gap on top of their own bottom margin.</li>
    <li><strong>Controls:</strong> every label spaces its own text, control and help, so the form needs no wrapper between fields.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<section id="footer">
  <h2>Site footer</h2>
  <p>
    Columns of links and a tagline, divided from the page by a rule.
  </p>
  {% set classless %}
<footer>
  <hr class="divider" />
  <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(9rem, 1fr)); gap: var(--padding)">
    <div>
      <strong>Sparkstone</strong>
      <p class="text-secondary"><small>Where technology sparks innovation</small></p>
    </div>
    <nav class="flex" style="flex-direction: column; gap: calc(var(--padding) / 4)">
      <a href="#" class="link">Case Studies</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">Products</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">Blog</a>
    </nav>
    <nav class="flex" style="flex-direction: column; gap: calc(var(--padding) / 4)">
      <a href="#" class="link">Free Tools</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">About</a>
      <a href="#" class="link">Book a call</a>
    </nav>
  </div>
</footer>
  {% endset %}
  {{ m.demo("studio-footer", classless) }}
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Layout:</strong> link columns are <code>.flex</code> turned on its side, with the gap brought down to a quarter of <code>--padding</code> so the links read as one list.</li>
    <li><strong>Surface:</strong> <code>.divider</code> is the same <code>--divider</code> rule as a card's border, so the footer belongs to the same family without being boxed in.</li>
  </ul>
</section>
{% endblock %}
